# Specialized Vulnerability Rule Dispatch

Ensures vulnerability-specific rules are ONLY loaded when the corresponding vulnerability type is discovered.

## Lightweight Discovery Rules (Load First)

### Serialization Usage Discovery
- Rule: 26-serialization-usage-discovery.mdc
- Always Load during initial analysis to check for serialization usage
- Triggers 25-deserialization-detection.mdc when serialization detected

### CORS Usage Discovery
- Rule: 27-cors-usage-discovery.mdc
- Triggers 08-cors-severity-assessment.mdc when CORS detected

## Conditional Rules (Load ONLY When Vulnerability Found)

### Deserialization Vulnerabilities
- Rule: 25-deserialization-detection.mdc
- Trigger: Serialization method signatures found, deserialization libraries present
- Skip When: No serialization usage detected

### SQL Injection
- Rule: 05-sql-injection-prevention.mdc
- Trigger: String concatenation in SQL queries, missing parameterized queries
- Skip When: No SQL database usage or all queries properly parameterized

### CORS Misconfiguration
- Rule: 08-cors-severity-assessment.mdc
- Trigger: CORS headers or middleware found
- Skip When: No CORS implementation detected

### XSS Prevention
- Rule: 06-xss-prevention.mdc
- Trigger: XSS vulnerability detected, unsafe HTML rendering
- Skip When: No web interface or all output properly encoded

### LLM Prompt Injection
- Rule: 04-llm-prompt-injection.mdc
- Trigger: LLM API integration detected (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.)
- Skip When: No LLM integration present

## Rule Loading Protocol

When a vulnerability is found, check if a specialized rule exists for it. If so, fetch and apply it. Otherwise use standard RAILGUARD remediation.

## Critical Constraints

NEVER load specialized rules during initial codebase discovery, as part of general analysis setup, or when the vulnerability type is not present.

ALWAYS load specialized rules after confirming vulnerability presence, when trigger conditions are met, and for accurate severity assessment.
